  "worked_solution": "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}y(6-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14094530/assets/4d5f6214785ff51d2254d8e9a2f7b15e811df643c3a664ee46b56744ede46e53.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=3y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis from $y=0$ to $y=6.$\nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}(3y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{6}y\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{6}-\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(36-0)-\\frac{1}{6}(216-0) \\\\  & =54-36 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
